1
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:58,500
If people never did silly things nothing intelligent would ever get done.

2
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Wittgenstein is a drama, history, comedy film directed by Derek Jarman.

3
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:43,500
A dramatization, in modern theatrical style, of the life and thought of the Viennese-born, Cambridge-educated
philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein, whose principal interest was the nature and limits of language.

4
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:36,000
A series of sketches depict the unfolding of his life from boyhood, through the era of the first World War, to his
eventual Cambridge professorship and association with Bertrand Russell and John Maynard Keynes.

5
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:28,500
The emphasis in these sketches is on the exposition of the ideas of Wittgenstein, a homosexual, and an
intuitive, moody, proud, and perfectionistic thinker generally regarded as a genius.

6
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Young Wittgenstein, played by Clancy Chassay.

7
00:20:07,500 --> 00:20:13,500
Ludwig Wittgenstein, played by Karl Johnson.

8
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:06,000
Betrand Russell, played by Michael Gough.

9
00:25:52,500 --> 00:25:58,500
Lady Ottoline Morrell, played by Tilda Swinton.

10
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:51,000
Johnny, played by Kevin Collins.

11
00:31:37,500 --> 00:31:43,500
Martian, played by Nabil Shaban.

12
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:36,000
Wittgenstein is a 1993 experimental comedy-drama film co-written and
directed by Derek Jarman, and produced by Tariq Ali.

13
00:37:22,500 --> 00:37:28,500
An international co-production of the United Kingdom and Japan, the film is loosely based on the
life story, as well as the philosophical thinking of philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein.

14
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:21,000
The adult Wittgenstein is played by Karl Johnson.

15
00:43:07,500 --> 00:43:13,500
The original screenplay by literary critic Terry Eagleton was heavily rewritten during pre-production and shooting by
Jarman, radically altering the style and structure, although retaining much of Eagleton's dialogue.

16
00:46:00,000 --> 00:46:06,000
The story is not played out in a traditional setting, but rather against a black backdrop
within which the actors and key props are placed, as if in a theatre setting.

17
00:48:52,500 --> 00:48:58,500
The film was originally part of a series of 12 films on the life and ideas
of philosophers, produced by Ali on behalf of Channel Four.

18
00:51:45,000 --> 00:51:51,000
Only four of the scripts got commissioned: Socrates by Howard Brenton,
Spinoza by Ali, Locke by David Edgar and Wittgenstein by Eagleton.

19
00:54:37,500 --> 00:54:43,500
Spinoza was filmed and directed by Chris Spencer as
Spinoza : The Apostle of Reason.

20
00:57:30,000 --> 00:57:36,000
Citizen Locke was filmed and directed by Agnieszka Piotrowska.

21
01:00:22,500 --> 01:00:28,500
These were broadcast in 1994 as 52-minute television films.

22
01:03:15,000 --> 01:03:21,000
The film explores themes of biography, lgbt, gay theme.

23
01:06:07,500 --> 01:06:13,500
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
